
%%
%% SECTION 1: INTRODUCTION
%%
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Large language models (LLMs) are now embedded in educational tools, clinical decision support,
legal research platforms, software engineering workflows, and everyday information retrieval. A
model that serves as a trusted interlocutor must exhibit a basic epistemic virtue: the willingness
to disagree with a user when the user is wrong. Sycophancy, named for the flatterer who tells
powerful people what they want to hear, is the systematic failure of this virtue: the model favors
the user's stated or implied position over the evidence.

The behavior was first systematically documented by \citet{sharma2023understanding}, who showed
that five state-of-the-art assistants exhibited sycophantic agreement across four free-form
generation tasks and traced the behavior in part to human preference judgments that reward
agreeable responses over accurate ones. The problem has since intensified as models are tuned for
warmth. \citet{ibrahim2026warm} show in controlled experiments across five models that training
for warm responses raises error rates by 10 to 30 percentage points and makes models approximately
40\% more likely to reinforce incorrect user beliefs, with the effect most pronounced when users
express sadness. At scale, \citet{cheng2025prosocial} demonstrate across 11 models
($N = 2{,}405$ participants) that AI affirms users' actions 49\% more often than humans do, that a
single sycophantic interaction reduces willingness to repair interpersonal conflicts, and that
sycophantic models are trusted and preferred despite distorting judgment. Published in
\emph{Nature} and \emph{Science} respectively, these findings position sycophancy as a mainstream
deployment risk rather than a narrow alignment technicality, and the risk has already been
realized operationally: in April 2025 OpenAI rolled back a GPT-4o update after it made the model
excessively agreeable in production~\citep{openai2025sycophancy}.

The connection to training method appears structural rather than incidental.
RLHF~\citep{christiano2017deep,ziegler2019finetuning,stiennon2020summarize} optimizes a policy
against a reward model trained on human preference judgments; if raters systematically prefer
agreeable responses, the pipeline amplifies agreement, and \citet{shapira2026rlhf} characterize
this amplification formally. Section~\ref{sec:causes} separates the controlled from the
associational evidence behind this pathway. Research has grown quickly around it: works with
sycophancy as a primary subject numbered 7 in 2023 and 179 between January and 20 August 2026
(Section~\ref{subsec:reftypes}).

\subsection{Why a Security Framing, and What This SoK Adds}
\label{subsec:positioning}

Prior consolidation efforts approach sycophancy as a model behavior.
\citet{malmqvist2024sycophancy} surveys causes and mitigations from an alignment perspective, and
\citet{ye2026taxonomy} systematize the construct itself through a review of 70 papers; their
two-axis taxonomy is the background we build on (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy}). Conceptual work has
since reframed the problem as premise governance in human-AI decisions~\citep{jain2026premise}, as
a boundary failure between social alignment and epistemic integrity~\citep{li2026boundary}, as
complacency rather than intentful flattery~\citep{germani2026complacent}, as a failure to surface
disagreement~\citep{vishwarupe2026pluralistic}, as a communication process~\citep{du2025alignment},
as one end of a misalignment taxonomy~\citep{shi2026deception}, and as a literature whose five
operationalizations never measure human perception~\citep{batzner2025claims}. None models an
adversary or assesses control coverage against a threat model, and none treats sycophancy as a
property of a deployed system on which a human decision depends. That gap leaves unstated who owns
the risk, what a mitigation must guarantee, against what adversary, and how assurance could be
certified. This SoK reads the literature the way a security engineer reads a vulnerability class:
asset, threat model, measurement instruments, controls, and residual risk. It makes four
contributions.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
  \item \textbf{A security framing.} We define the protected asset as the integrity of the human
    decision, not the model, and give a threat model in which the prompt is an unvalidated input
    channel exploitable without any breach, separating adversarial manipulation from
    non-adversarial reliability failure (Section~\ref{sec:threatmodel}).
  \item \textbf{Typed measurement from a reproducible corpus.} From a reproducible database search
    we code all 44 dedicated instruments by the reference against which they judge sycophancy,
    show that the four references are incommensurable, and identify what the instruments can and
    cannot certify (Section~\ref{sec:measurement}).
  \item \textbf{Graded controls as defense in depth.} We grade the evidence behind 36 mitigations
    across data, training, inference, and governance tiers, cross them with the threat model, and
    expose coverage gaps, including displacement of suppressed sycophancy into forms audits
    cannot see (Section~\ref{sec:controls}).
  \item \textbf{An assurance-oriented research agenda.} We identify open problems that block
    certification, and specify a longitudinal study design for epistemic entrainment, the
    possibility that exposure itself erodes the user vigilance on which deployed controls depend
    (Section~\ref{sec:openproblems}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Corpus and Method of Systematization}
\label{subsec:method}

The corpus has two parts. The first is 62 sources spanning 2016 to 2026, assembled by snowball
citation chasing, forward and backward, from the foundational behavioral audits
\citep{sharma2023understanding,perez2023discovering}, together with a sweep for dedicated
benchmarks; it also supplies the adjacent constructs the argument requires (preference
optimization, reward hacking, unfaithful reasoning). The second is a reproducible database search
(Appendix~\ref{app:search}): titles or abstracts matching \texttt{sycophan*} in DataCite, the
registry that assigns every arXiv paper its DOI, run on 29 October 2026 and restricted to records
registered by 20 August 2026, which yields 423 records. Screening retained 277 and excluded 146 (141
passing mentions, 3 non-AI, 2 other). The first screening pass was LLM-assisted under a fixed
written protocol that requires a verbatim title or abstract quote for every decision; every
instrument, every mitigation, and every record bearing on a claim in this paper was then
hand-adjudicated, with full-text reading where the abstract did not settle the question. The query
returns 33 of the 49 arXiv papers in the snowball corpus, and all 16 misses are background or
adjacent-construct papers whose titles and abstracts never name sycophancy. The search adds 34
dedicated instruments, 21 sycophancy-specific mitigations, and 7 conceptual papers to the snowball
corpus. Every source was checked against its primary record (publisher page, proceedings index,
DataCite or arXiv record, or vendor site), and each is coded for construct, elicitation, modality,
control tier, and evidence status; the coding, the screening decisions, and a validator that fails
when either drifts from this paper's tables form the artifact (Appendix~\ref{app:openscience}). The
boundary should be read accordingly: identification is arXiv-only and English-language, and much
of the corpus consists of preprints evaluated on rapidly superseded model versions, a limitation
Section~\ref{sec:synthesis} weighs explicitly.
